cff-version: 1.2.0
title: >-
  Qizil: A Peephole and Commutation Optimizer for Quantum
  Intermediate Representation
message: >-
  If you use this software, or results produced with it, please cite it.
  Please also state whether a figure comes from the shipped examples or
  from the randomized test suite — they measure different things.
type: software
authors:
  - given-names: Egemen
    family-names: Sarıaslan
version: 0.2.0
date-released: '2026-08-27'
license: MIT
repository-code: 'https://github.com/egemensariaslan/qizil'
abstract: >-
  Qizil reads QIR (the LLVM-based intermediate representation Q#, Qiskit
  and PennyLane all target), builds a per-block quantum instruction DAG,
  and applies four peephole passes -- cancellation, rotation fusion,
  commutation-based reordering and Clifford+T resynthesis -- to a fixed
  point. Every rewrite is a closed-form algebraic identity, and every
  optimization run can be checked against two independent reference
  simulators for exact unitary equivalence up to a tracked global phase.
  On a 5-qubit Quantum Fourier Transform immediately followed by its own
  exact inverse, the optimizer collapses all 94 gates to 0, confirmed to
  1.44e-15 by the reference simulator. The equivalence checker itself is
  cross-checked against an independently implemented dense-embedding
  oracle, which caught a real operand-ordering bug before it shipped.
notes: >-
  Capstone project for the Microsoft AI/ML Summer Internship Programme,
  2026. A capstone project produced during the programme; not affiliated
  with or endorsed by Microsoft Corporation.
keywords:
  - quantum computing
  - compiler optimization
  - QIR
  - fault-tolerant quantum computing
  - formal verification
  - peephole optimization
  - LLVM
